Imagine your Linux system is a big tree.

The starting point of the tree is:

/

This / is called root directory.

Everything in Linux is organized underneath this /.

Think about Windows:

C:\
 ├── Windows
 ├── Users
 ├── Program Files
 └── ...

Linux doesn't normally start with C:\.

Linux starts with:

/

Then everything comes underneath it.

/
├── bin
├── boot
├── dev
├── etc
├── home
├── lib
├── opt
├── root
├── sbin
├── tmp
├── usr
└── var

1. / — Root directory

This is the top of the entire Linux filesystem.

It is not the same thing as the root user.

For example:

cd /
ls

You will see directories such as:

bin
boot
dev
etc
home
lib
opt
root
sbin
tmp
usr
var

Think:
/ = entire Linux filesystem

Everything starts from here.

⚠️ Very important: / vs /root

This confuses almost every beginner.

/

Means:

The top/main directory of the entire filesystem.

/root

Means:

The home directory of the root user.

So:

/       → Main filesystem
/root   → root user's personal home
/home   → Normal users' personal homes

Don't think /root means "the main root directory."

2. /home — Normal users' homes

Suppose your Linux machine has three normal users:

bilal
user1
user2

Linux can have:

/home
├── bilal
├── user1
└── user2

For example:

/home/bilal

is Bilal's home directory.

You can keep things like:

Documents
Downloads
Pictures
Scripts
Projects

there.

For example:

cd /home
ls

You might see:

bilal
user1

Then:

cd /home/bilal

Now you are inside Bilal's home directory.

Simple memory:
/home = normal users' houses

Each user gets their own "room/house."

3. /root — Root user's home

Linux has a special administrator account called:

root

The root user's home directory is:

/root

For example:

cd /root

if you have permission.

So:

/root

contains the root user's personal files/configuration.

Remember this:
/       → Root of filesystem
/root   → Home of root user

They are completely different concepts.

4. /etc — Configuration ⭐⭐⭐

This is very important for a Linux administrator.

Think of /etc as the configuration cupboard of Linux.

It contains configuration files for:

Linux system
Services
Applications
Networking
Users
Storage
SSH
etc.

Examples:

/etc/hosts
/etc/passwd
/etc/fstab
/etc/ssh/

For example:

cat /etc/hosts

You may see:

127.0.0.1 localhost

Another example:

ls /etc/ssh

You may see SSH configuration files.

5. /var — Data that changes frequently ⭐⭐⭐

var means variable.

The idea is:

Data stored here changes frequently while the system is running.

Examples include:

Logs
Cache
Application data
Spool data
Databases/application-related changing data

One of the most important directories is:

/var/log

This contains system/application logs.

For example:

cd /var/log
ls

You may see files/directories such as:

journal
apt
auth.log
syslog

depending on your Linux distribution.

6. /tmp — Temporary files

tmp means temporary.

Programs and users can use /tmp to store temporary data.

For example:

cd /tmp
ls

You may find temporary files created by applications.

Think of /tmp like a temporary working table.

You put something there temporarily, use it, and eventually it can be cleaned up.

7. /boot — Files needed to boot Linux

When you switch on your computer, Linux has to start/boot.

The /boot directory contains important files used during that process.

For example:

/boot/vmlinuz...
/boot/initrd...

You may see files related to:

Linux kernel
Initial RAM filesystem
Bootloader configuration/files

The Linux kernel is commonly represented by files beginning with:

vmlinuz

For example:

ls /boot

You might see:

vmlinuz-...
initrd.img-...

8. /dev — Devices ⭐⭐⭐

This is a very important Linux concept.

dev means devices.

Linux represents many hardware devices and special devices as files under /dev.

For example:

/dev/sda
/dev/nvme0n1
/dev/null

You might think:

"Why is a disk represented as a file?"

That's part of the Linux philosophy:

Many things are represented through files/interfaces.

For example, a disk might appear as:

/dev/sda

An NVMe disk might appear as:

/dev/nvme0n1

And /dev/null is a special device that essentially discards data written to it.

Try:

ls /dev

You'll see many device entries.

9. /usr — Programs, libraries and shared files

This one is confusing because the name /usr can make you think:

"Is this where user files are stored?"

No.

Normal users' personal files are generally under:

/home

/usr is mainly a major location for programs, libraries and other system/shareable files.

For example:

/usr/bin
/usr/lib
/usr/share

You will find many installed programs under:

/usr/bin

For example:

ls /usr/bin

You'll see many commands/programs.

10. /bin — Essential commands

bin means binary.

Historically, /bin contained essential executable commands.

Examples include commands such as:

ls
cp
mv
cat

You can check:

ls /bin
But there is an important modern Linux detail ⭐

On many modern Linux distributions, /bin is a symbolic link to:

/usr/bin

You can check:

ls -ld /bin

You may see something similar to:

/bin -> usr/bin

So don't be surprised if /bin and /usr/bin appear connected.

11. /sbin — System administration commands

sbin historically means system binaries.

It contains commands primarily associated with system administration.

For example, commands/tools related to:

Disk management
Networking
System configuration
System administration

You can check:

ls /sbin

Again, on many modern Linux distributions:

/sbin

is also linked into /usr.

You can check:

ls -ld /sbin

11. /lib — Libraries

lib means libraries.

Programs often need libraries to perform certain operations.

Think about a program as a person who wants to do some work.

Instead of every program creating everything itself, it can use libraries that provide functionality.

So:

/lib

contains important libraries needed by programs and the system.

On modern Linux systems, /lib may also be a symbolic link into /usr/lib.

You can check:

ls -ld /lib

13. /opt — Optional / third-party software

opt is commonly used for optional software, particularly additional or third-party applications.

For example, an organization might install an application like:

/opt/application/

You might see software installed under:

/opt/

Note:- One very important thing: directory vs file

Look at this:

/etc/ssh/sshd_config

Break it down:

/
└── etc
    └── ssh
        └── sshd_config

Here:

/               → root directory
/etc             → configuration directory
/etc/ssh         → SSH configuration directory
/etc/ssh/sshd_config → SSH configuration file


